A||N||5||Seanh||Rattle can paint jobs work?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-15-2001||12:20 PM||||Well, latly my car has been a magnet for shopping carts, kids on bikes, various rocks, and other drivers. I need to repaint the rear bumper, gound F/X, and passenger side outter door skin. I don't really have the resources to do this, nor the know-how. So, I was wondering how I should do it? Should I buy some automotive paint? I have a compressor and paint spray gun, but have never painted autobody before. Or should I just use rattle can paint? Do I sand the panel down to the surface, or just ruff the top paint layer up a bit. Primer it? How many coats of paint to apply? Then, clear coating? Please give me pretty detailed instructions on what to do, I'm pretty dumb when it comes to painting this. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mr.Frosty||06-15-2001||12:35 PM||||I would say go the compressor/gun route.I too have both,but when it comes to the reducer/hardener stages I'm lost.I know how to shoot it,but haven't a clue about modern painting procedures involving those additives.I want to reshoot my roof and deck sooo bad.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-15-2001||12:39 PM||||Don't feel bad, I didn't even know there were reducer/hardner stages  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||01:31 PM||||there was a thread about a week ago on rattle can painting - lots of people have gotten good results with it<P>  try searching on 'rattle can' up there with the search icon<P>I would get a book from the library or bookstore on spray painting and get a handle on whats involved with using the compressor and gun - there are several adjustments to be controlled, and each one will cause the paint to come out 'less than ideal' if its off - so when you are spraying and you start to get orangepeel, for example, you will need to know what to change on the setup.<P> Also if you can find someone that will walk you through the setup and your first few layers of paint, all the better.<P>  if you look into it and it all sounds too confusing, then using spray cans is simpler, fewer variables to control. depending on how much of your car you want to paint, spray cans could be cheaper in the end, compaired to buying quarts of paint, thinner, getting your compressor all cleaned up and running nice, practicing with it...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||01:39 PM||||oh -one last thing - if you use spray cans on a small area you can get away with a paper mask, or none if you dont mind having silver boogers for a week (or whatever color your car is).<P>  but if you use the compressor, the paint and thinner is sprayed out in a much finer mist, and you really NEED an organic resperator, the ones that look like a gas mask, and cost $40 or more.<P>  in fact, some paints WILL kill you if you spray them without a resperator.<P>  Its a good investment though. With the organic filters on it you cant smell stuff with it on, literally, you can put the mask on and open a bottle of fingernail polish remover and dump the whole thing on your shirt, and you wont smell it at all.<P>  I used mine about a month ago when I realized there was a dead squirrel just above the flue in the chimney of my fireplace. It had been in there for a few months and the maggots had already gotten to it (thats how I knew it was up there - where are all these flys coming from?!)<P>   it was bad enought having to pick it out with the surgical latex gloves on and having to see it. Im glad I didnt have to smell it too. (c%||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||06-15-2001||02:00 PM||||Eastwood Company may have the perfect middle ground.  They have propellant cans - basically an aerosol can with a cup attachment.  You buy your bulk paint and fill the cup.  Then use it just like a rattle can.  That way you get the exact paint you need, and it's more suited to jobs that are too big for spray cans and don't quite require the compressor and gun.  Check them out at <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodcompany.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eastwoodcompany.com</A>  . I highly recommmend getting their catalog.  Lots of great stuff for painting, plating, powder coating, body work, etc.  All of this stuff that can be done at home, too!  Excellent resource. ||||reg||6
